#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0c2486d6-bb55-52ba-9fd8-6b516d3f7858" :question/math-academy-id "e-5557" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors such that $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 6$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}+4\\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 25.$  If $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v},$ find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v},$ we also have that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $4\\mathbf{v}.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+‖4v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+(4‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+16‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (25)^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+16(6)^{2} \\\\ 625 & =‖u{‖}^{2}+576 \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =625-576 \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}=\\Vert\\mathbf{u}\\Vert^2=49.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "112253ae-e0ea-554a-83bf-87f8ba7c9ce7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5558" :question/problem "Given that $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} +3\\mathbf{v} \\Vert=14$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert=2,$ find the smallest possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}\\Vert.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+‖3v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+3‖v‖ \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖+3(2) \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖+6 \\\\ 8 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$ is $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133507 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "5e72235b-a419-5f27-bee7-01be8b49c997" :question/math-academy-id "e-5559" :question/problem "Let the vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}|^2+5\\Vert\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\Vert^2=29$ and $\\| \\mathbf{u} \\|  = \\| \\mathbf{w} \\| = 2$, then what is the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert?$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =4‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =4‖v{‖}^{2}+5(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & \\le 4‖v{‖}^{2}+5(‖v{‖}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & \\le 9‖v{‖}^{2}+20 \\\\ 9 & \\le 9‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2$ implies that $1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$. Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$ is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cacf49f-2987-5051-843a-15243f843408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327091} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6e7fcb-4608-552a-9191-e5c31c7e2a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327092} {:answer/id #uuid "c50df9bb-f947-500d-9a87-62abf5d5d42e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327093} {:answer/id #uuid "7ece6b57-0545-5672-a39a-dc33bea35c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327094} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbb2a6e-630a-5ba0-99a4-dc6245198010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fbb2a6e-630a-5ba0-99a4-dc6245198010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327095} :answer-field/id #uuid "1da710da-d5c1-5de2-88e8-a9bf6b296374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b890941-b213-56f5-bd82-e5a5ff2f26e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-140462" :question/problem "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2}=204$ and $‖u‖=4$ and $‖w‖=7$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}204 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3(‖v{‖}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =19‖v{‖}^{2}+147.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}204 & \\le 19‖v‖+147 \\\\ 57 & \\le 19‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 3 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $3\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $\\sqrt{3}\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52c3b15-7f3a-54cf-b50d-d344d26b8685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{337}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131289} {:answer/id #uuid "e102a30a-58e9-56ca-b603-3fd3d685511b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131290} {:answer/id #uuid "e473836c-095d-522d-9a46-80145fe4328d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131291} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2492ad-f832-557b-a75e-8ae82d578300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131292} {:answer/id #uuid "4be2ca3b-dea8-5f2c-ab94-4eac8e635fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e102a30a-58e9-56ca-b603-3fd3d685511b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131290} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa37048-9b16-5f4d-905e-b7cd97665ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78f436e6-da8d-594d-9217-c77afac12ee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-39412" :question/problem "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖v‖=7$ and $‖2u+v‖=25.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖u‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $2u$ is orthogonal to $v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2u+v{‖}^{2} & =‖2u{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2u+v{‖}^{2} & =(2‖u‖)^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖2u+v{‖}^{2} & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (25)^{2} & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+(7)^{2} \\\\ 625 & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+49 \\\\ 576 & =4‖u{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖u‖=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d44920-f873-5adb-b000-d3d0355ece07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131295} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d72c27-90a4-5bd0-99cf-21d21242717c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131296} {:answer/id #uuid "fd55b552-b8b5-5e7e-9b45-5f65597d0629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131297} {:answer/id #uuid "38976d3f-cb14-5958-af40-bafdb4ee4cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131298} {:answer/id #uuid "754b8be4-fcc2-5cc6-b1d4-fb1cdb99d7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5d72c27-90a4-5bd0-99cf-21d21242717c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131296} :answer-field/id #uuid "e76f3735-5aa0-5f7e-8fb0-79d7db199c6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0646260-da28-5d60-b7dd-5d44630043ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-39428" :question/problem "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖v‖=5$ and $‖2u+3v‖=17.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖u‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $2u$ is orthogonal to $3v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2u+3v{‖}^{2} & =‖2u{‖}^{2}+‖3v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2u+3v{‖}^{2} & =(2‖u‖)^{2}+(3‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖2u+3v{‖}^{2} & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+9‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (17)^{2} & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+9(5)^{2} \\\\ 289 & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 289 & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+225 \\\\ 64 & =4‖u{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖u‖=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad0b60a4-08fa-552b-baa7-d6379986f121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309727} {:answer/id #uuid "d1590ba7-c219-5ad9-8fab-b1cda1d515d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309728} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6df9e1-b24b-5691-b671-ba53374460f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309729} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4d1931-6c52-538c-8023-d0ede89415aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309730} {:answer/id #uuid "290ad22d-0376-59fb-9e5b-f653f01d558c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "290ad22d-0376-59fb-9e5b-f653f01d558c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309731} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a40b6ea-2245-512f-ba22-45998bc16202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c491862-6cc2-5142-91ba-7a0b72655537" :question/math-academy-id "q-39608" :question/problem "If $|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖=34$ and $‖v‖=6$, then what is the smallest possible value of $‖u‖?$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & =|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =‖u‖\\cdot (6)+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =6‖u‖+‖u+v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the triangle inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6‖u‖+‖u+v‖ & \\le 6‖u‖+(‖u‖+‖v‖) \\\\  & =6‖u‖+(‖u‖+6) \\\\  & =7‖u‖+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two inequalities, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & \\le 7‖u‖+6 \\\\ 28 & \\le 7‖u‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87df2cbd-04c9-5c28-9c2b-0eff9b60932a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131577} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d13305-5f5a-5fb3-9862-2eac9a4cc291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131578} {:answer/id #uuid "178a02a2-ae89-5d15-93a2-1a2b23dcffbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131579} {:answer/id #uuid "4dae6979-bdf2-541c-8269-340828d9b8fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131580} {:answer/id #uuid "6857b418-4530-5ab8-a43c-dff7a5af22ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6857b418-4530-5ab8-a43c-dff7a5af22ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131581} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd80b7ca-eb31-56ac-a2be-529425354a72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "138b1b9d-8498-5ed8-934a-4cf69b7bdd6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40197" :question/problem "Given that $‖4u+v‖=22$ and $‖v‖=2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le ‖4u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le 4‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 22 & \\le 4‖u‖+2 \\\\ 20 & \\le 4‖u‖ \\\\ 5 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b35cbf-5529-51a5-bcc1-557a65d58c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327098} {:answer/id #uuid "dae480ff-090f-5697-897d-db490c2677b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327099} {:answer/id #uuid "657f1701-472e-5f66-9fb0-790a0ebddd04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327100} {:answer/id #uuid "67e6cc85-4bae-5922-8fb1-262742215a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} {:answer/id #uuid "b9cabd05-1c3a-515c-8980-dba9fb8a594b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "657f1701-472e-5f66-9fb0-790a0ebddd04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327100} :answer-field/id #uuid "777f9c3f-2deb-540b-a837-731ef9a1e092" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb63f147-46bc-5152-9034-eaf5b3486fb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-40242" :question/problem "If $|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖=12\\sqrt{3}$ and $‖v‖=2\\sqrt{3}$, then what is the smallest possible value of $‖u‖?$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{3} & =|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =‖u‖\\cdot (2\\sqrt{3})+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}‖u‖+‖u+v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the triangle inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}‖u‖+‖u+v‖ & \\le 2\\sqrt{3}‖u‖+(‖u‖+‖v‖) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}‖u‖+(‖u‖+2\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{3}+1)‖u‖+2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two inequalities, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{3} & \\le (2\\sqrt{3}+1)‖u‖+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 10\\sqrt{3} & \\le (2\\sqrt{3}+1)‖u‖ \\\\ \\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}+1} & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}+1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe8a947-6153-58fe-b65f-ad4219945521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309734} {:answer/id #uuid "acc98e97-cd60-54e8-a31f-9e57b454e8a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309735} {:answer/id #uuid "38da9464-c2aa-510f-bfc5-ce507c75360a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309736} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa53caf-fe66-5ffe-9d17-2e552d6995c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309737} {:answer/id #uuid "c321f81d-98f4-54ba-83a4-ff4ce0b89455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38da9464-c2aa-510f-bfc5-ce507c75360a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309736} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c4c843-e8e8-5f58-ab16-fd3ed9cb0715" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47488a47-14a3-5678-954b-f24ebcdf0f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40243" :question/problem "If $|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖=19$ and $‖v‖=9$, then what is the smallest possible value of $‖u‖?$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & =|u\\cdot v|+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =‖u‖\\cdot (9)+‖u+v‖ \\\\  & =9‖u‖+‖u+v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the triangle inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9‖u‖+‖u+v‖ & \\le 9‖u‖+(‖u‖+‖v‖) \\\\  & =9‖u‖+(‖u‖+9) \\\\  & =10‖u‖+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two inequalities, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & \\le 10‖u‖+9 \\\\ 10 & \\le 10‖u‖ \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125af8d5-bf83-5106-a1ae-303b53449930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131643} {:answer/id #uuid "295fe950-b638-54e3-b7f4-badeb7a832f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131644} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd215e9-b290-5424-a3b8-f31f4d439c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131645} {:answer/id #uuid "483c7c4a-d2f0-5f4e-868a-c08d561e4b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131646} {:answer/id #uuid "7330b971-0d9e-5159-b9f7-94ab74ceebc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7330b971-0d9e-5159-b9f7-94ab74ceebc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131647} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa32156d-18c0-5bd2-b442-3977cc1a663e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b171c49-12bc-5a9e-82c9-5de048b43a17" :question/math-academy-id "q-40244" :question/problem "Given that $‖7u+v‖=32$ and $‖v‖=4$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖7u+v‖ & \\le ‖7u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖7u+v‖ & \\le 7‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 32 & \\le 7‖u‖+4 \\\\ 28 & \\le 7‖u‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5bc08a9-b6e4-54bc-b4aa-017bd50a47f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309741} {:answer/id #uuid "72e53761-85c7-5709-8eac-8b72e55471e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309742} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1689fd-76be-5cec-ae3f-b7cd3071ad95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309743} {:answer/id #uuid "ef791f48-0f71-5920-95e1-d516d1c7c224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309744} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2e85a1-ccd1-5d42-b480-f714b3135378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef791f48-0f71-5920-95e1-d516d1c7c224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309744} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b1900a-9bd6-5d03-b339-2a38dcac2bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de123b7e-6906-5d9f-ab1d-3d3f8fdc4d35" :question/math-academy-id "q-40263" :question/problem "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖u‖=6\\sqrt{2}$ and $‖u+2v‖=6\\sqrt{6}.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖v‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $u$ is orthogonal to $2v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+2v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+‖2v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+2v{‖}^{2} & =‖u‖+(‖2v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖u+2v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+4‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (6\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}+4‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 216 & =72+4‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 144 & =4‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖v{‖}^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖v‖=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da4cff1c-633c-5daa-b2c6-a12b1dfe29fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309748} {:answer/id #uuid "a332669e-4c33-5a9a-b676-e08755f41618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309749} {:answer/id #uuid "b15d5cb9-0771-5882-9b52-9799560c4883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309750} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5cf131-5b5c-59dc-8868-3fbb8073e006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1\\,013}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309751} {:answer/id #uuid "4c852026-e34a-5359-a25c-c27a20ddb5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a332669e-4c33-5a9a-b676-e08755f41618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309749} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae948f1d-79d0-56c9-b205-f6111f5db881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fd4f0c8-283c-51b8-8f02-c3a374eace40" :question/math-academy-id "q-40264" :question/problem "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖u‖=15$ and $‖u+\\sqrt{5}v‖=75.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖v‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $u$ is orthogonal to $\\sqrt{5}v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+\\sqrt{5}v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+‖\\sqrt{5}v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+\\sqrt{5}v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5}‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖u+\\sqrt{5}v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+5‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (75)^{2} & =(15)^{2}+5‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 5\\,625 & =225+5‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 5\\,400 & =5‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖v{‖}^{2} & =1\\,080\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖v‖=6\\sqrt{30}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381d9aa7-b698-568f-be67-e9d0e99fe2fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327105} {:answer/id #uuid "7ade5330-5789-5e7d-8e71-9107541d6ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327106} {:answer/id #uuid "59063544-b2b6-5e88-a659-c60c70adac73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327107} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c1c52b-a788-53b7-a2a8-6d40a7a2a774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327108} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7d5d15-c297-580d-91ce-fbaeee2a21de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e7d5d15-c297-580d-91ce-fbaeee2a21de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327109} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f480a6d-e13d-5e5b-b53b-33068efcf578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4b83d81-ba31-544f-aa9b-33ae4c0375b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40898" :question/problem "Given that $‖6u+v‖=11$ and $‖v‖=2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖6u+v‖ & \\le ‖6u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖6u+v‖ & \\le 6‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 11 & \\le 6‖u‖+2 \\\\ 9 & \\le 6‖u‖ \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2d92642-32c8-56d0-b40f-33ee82fd5531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327112} {:answer/id #uuid "d072ad69-93e9-52a1-afc8-d0a5704d03d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327113} {:answer/id #uuid "de3edb5a-d179-5629-89b8-495b8b6708b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327114} {:answer/id #uuid "6fee3d5c-3d75-5da1-ba0c-96e138e1d310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327115} {:answer/id #uuid "bca72d18-fecb-5744-bd39-e1d5b49cea90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3edb5a-d179-5629-89b8-495b8b6708b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327114} :answer-field/id #uuid "116100a7-46f9-560c-b49c-389696618870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b086fe32-6449-5a47-a5ac-e33d2ec31ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40901" :question/problem "Given that $‖9u+v‖=23$ and $‖v‖=5$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖9u+v‖ & \\le ‖9u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖9u+v‖ & \\le 9‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 23 & \\le 9‖u‖+5 \\\\ 18 & \\le 9‖u‖ \\\\ 2 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cabfc52-f16c-52f4-83d6-89f91b8adc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309769} {:answer/id #uuid "51c545b0-5640-5a8f-b657-82a3bf9a1cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309770} {:answer/id #uuid "e39e7ed4-bb25-53c3-8a5a-54ce81da77fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309771} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7b3e3c-358c-59b3-a09c-2842c5975b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309772} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d34f44-0556-5d04-9e90-e996f721860a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0d34f44-0556-5d04-9e90-e996f721860a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a177a4-bc16-578c-974a-ea68efc9c133" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9c77086-5264-54dd-9623-65feabc3a826" :question/math-academy-id "q-74109" :question/problem "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖u‖=4$ and $‖\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v‖=\\sqrt{182}.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖v‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $\\sqrt{2}u$ is orthogonal to $\\sqrt{3}v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{‖}^{2} & =‖\\sqrt{2}u{‖}^{2}+‖\\sqrt{3}v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{‖}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{2}‖u‖)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{‖}^{2} & =2‖u{‖}^{2}+3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (\\sqrt{182})^{2} & =2(4)^{2}+3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 182 & =2\\cdot 16+3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 182 & =32+3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 150 & =3‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖v{‖}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖v‖=5\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd91940e-f509-5a42-a014-4fac899a9916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309776} {:answer/id #uuid "bb50243f-f27a-5732-ad22-fdb761c767b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309777} {:answer/id #uuid "166b41b6-702c-5823-9970-5e6c4876a760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309778} {:answer/id #uuid "d22db3fc-4ed3-5008-9174-cb97b678adff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309779} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc2ad41-9941-5d2c-99dd-ecf427cd2971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd91940e-f509-5a42-a014-4fac899a9916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309776} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9bc8519-5c29-5b44-a192-95af85774026" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61513b82-911a-5ff9-bb4e-85b029111b65" :question/math-academy-id "q-74177" :question/problem "Given that $‖u+5v‖=19$ and $‖u‖=4$, find the smallest possible value of $‖v‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+5v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+‖5v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+5v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+5‖v‖ \\\\ 19 & \\le 4+5‖v‖ \\\\ 15 & \\le 5‖v‖ \\\\ 3 & \\le ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖v‖$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf41f401-e0dd-5e32-bdaa-9fc27ac52864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327119} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ea1f7-4bda-57b0-8037-cf6086307d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327120} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa45136-9e3d-58c6-93eb-16148c84a764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327121} {:answer/id #uuid "09cb2125-ab2e-50a7-b8fc-dd863601f451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327122} {:answer/id #uuid "09c3a527-8da8-5642-9de9-1b9667b694f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aa45136-9e3d-58c6-93eb-16148c84a764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327121} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e7400e-0173-5326-a9d8-237cca1079a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac7d1f8c-831e-5e15-b329-401bcc807fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-74193" :question/problem "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2}=158$ and $‖u‖=‖w‖=5$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}158 & ={|u\\cdot v|}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25‖v{‖}^{2}+2‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & =25‖v{‖}^{2}+2(‖v{‖}^{2}+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =27‖v{‖}^{2}+50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}158 & \\le 27‖v‖+50 \\\\ 108 & \\le 27‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $4\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $2\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $2.$"}]}
